Big O notation is used to describe the complexity of an algorithm, that is, the upper bound of the algorithm's execution time as the amount of input data grows. Below is an example illustrating how to construct a Big O notation to explain the complexity of a certain algorithm.

Suppose there is a simple algorithm for calculating the sum of an integer array:

```
function sum(arr) {
    let total = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        total += arr[i];
    }
    return total;
}
```

This algorithm takes an integer array of length n as input. The for loop in the algorithm executes n times, and each iteration of the loop contains only one statement, namely `total += arr[i];`, whose execution time is constant time. Therefore, the execution time of this algorithm is n times constant time, that is, O(n).

When constructing Big O notation, we usually ignore constant time and lower-order terms, focusing only on the upper bound of the algorithm's execution time as the amount of input data grows, in order to better describe the algorithm's time complexity.

In the example above, we can say that the complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n represents the length of the input array.

